      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Lipid blood fractions, malonic dialdehyde and antioxidant activity of plasma and red blood cells were evaluated in 56 patients with type I diabetes mellitus. Twenty-three of those had coronary heart disease. It was established that, if the patients received mean doses of insulin 80.5 +/- 2.70 U/day, they had higher levels of cholesterol, triglycerides and VLDL cholesterol than those who were treated with insulin in a dose of 43.2 +/- 1.84 U/day."},
